  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 21,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.479,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Measuring Space: Perimeter and Area"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "139"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.107,
            0.886,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If we imagine a perpendicular dropped from B to the base CD, its length (recall the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem) is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt{c^{2}-\\left ( b-a \\right ) ^{2} }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Hence, the area is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a + b ) h"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We get exactly the same formula if we recall that the area of an isosceles trapezium is half the sum of the parallel sides times the distance between the parallel sides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.238,
            0.851,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Special Cases and Generalisation in Mathematics"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.276,
            0.855,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The notion of a ‘special case’ occurs often in higher mathematics. A special case results from a general result whenever we apply some extra condition. We list a few such examples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.336,
            0.855,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The general result is also called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "generalisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the special case. The process of generalisation is an extremely important part of mathematics."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.378,
            0.629,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A square is a special case of a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.402,
            0.854,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, the formula for the area of a square "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( A = a ^ { 2 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a special case of the formula for the area of a rectangle "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(A = ab)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " obtained by putting "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "b = a ,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.444,
            0.855,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, the formula for the perimeter of a square is a special case of the formula for the perimeter of a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.485,
            0.853,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " An isosceles right-angled triangle is a special case of a right-angled triangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.528,
            0.854,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, from any theorem about all right-angled triangles, we can extract a special case that applies to isosceles, right-angled triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.569,
            0.855,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a right-angled triangle ABC with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle C = 90^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ; then we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (this is the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem). Let us take a special case of this with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a = b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (this corresponds to the triangle being isosceles). We get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a ^ { 2 } \\quad + \\quad b ^ { 2 } \\quad = \\quad c ^ { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "c^{2} = 2a^{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "c = a { \\sqrt { 2 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The general theorem "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = c ^ { 2 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has reduced to this special case "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( c = a { \\sqrt { 2 } } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for isosceles right-angled triangles (with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a = b )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.708,
            0.854,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here is another example. Compare the following identities from algebra:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.733,
            0.365,
            0.763
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 2ab,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.773,
            0.554,
            0.805
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\left(a+b+c\\right)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2ab+2bc+2ca."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.81,
            0.856,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you see that the first identity is a special case of the second one (put "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "c = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " in the second identity), and the second identity is a generalisation of the first one?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.87,
            0.855,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You will meet many examples of special cases and generalisation in later years."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
